\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Sequential limits and the relativistic conclusion}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{The construction has two successive limits. The finite periodic
law supplies an upper bound for two-point forms; it is not identified
with the limiting half-Dirichlet law. The diagram distinguishes that
comparison from the limits and reconstruction.}

\tikzfigs{0.82}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  object/.style={draw,rounded corners,align=center,
    minimum width=29mm,minimum height=11mm,inner sep=3pt},
  every node/.style={font=\small},
  arrow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[object] (finite) at (0,4) {Finite square\\state};
\node[object] (square) at (5,4) {Continuum square\\state};
\node[object] (torus) at (0,0) {Periodic\\comparison};
\node[object] (euclidean) at (5,0) {Infinite-volume\\Euclidean state};
\node[object] (quantum) at (5,-4) {Wightman field\\unique vacuum};
\draw[arrow] (finite) -- (square);
\node[align=center] at (2.5,5.1) {$\epsilon\to0$\\$\ell$ fixed};
\draw[arrow] (square) -- (euclidean);
\node[align=right,anchor=east] at (4.65,2) {$\ell\to\infty$\\tests fixed};
\draw[arrow,dashed] (finite) -- (torus);
\node[align=right,anchor=east] at (-0.35,2) {two-point\\upper bound};
\draw[arrow] (euclidean) -- (quantum);
\node[align=right,anchor=east] at (4.65,-2) {A-C\\and mixing};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The first arrow is \ref{fdip-00I4}, the second
\ref{fdip-00I6}, and the dashed comparison is
\ref{fdip-00IC}. The last arrow uses all the constructive
regularity in \ref{fdip-00I8}, mixing in \ref{fdip-00IE}
and \citet{Theorem 2.6}{frohlich1974verification}.}

\p{The finite-chain [real-time evolution](fdip-00H8) is unitary
on its finite-spatial-volume Hilbert space. The stronger
Lorentz-covariant unitarity in \ref{fdip-00IH} comes from
the continuum Poincaré representation and its forward-cone spectrum.
The present conclusions give qualitative clustering and a unique
vacuum. They supply no exponential clustering rate, interacting
spectral mass gap, scattering theory or S-matrix. The positive
#{m_0} is the bare mass of the reference covariance.}
